    "raw_content_or_summary": "The Heidelberg University Library page provides 
bibliographic and access information for Arthur J. Evans' 1925 work \"The ring 
of Nestor\". The full text is accessible via PDF and page images. The contents 
are structured into two main sections: (I) \"Sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" (pp. 1–42), and (II) \"A 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world\" (pp. 43–75), followed by plates I–V. The 
work analyzes a group of gold signet rings and bead seals found at Thisbê in 
Boeotia, interpreted by Evans as a sepulchral treasure associated with Minoan 
iconography and beliefs. Within the first section, Evans describes the 
circumstances of the discovery of the treasure at Thisbê, including its location
in Boeotia, the nature of the burial context, and the association of the 
material with a tomb. He details the archaeologists and local authorities 
involved in bringing the treasure to scholarly attention, and gives 
measurements, descriptions, and iconographic analyses of the individual rings, 
among which the so‑called \"Ring of Nestor\" features prominently. Evans 
situates the rings within broader Minoan and Mycenaean cultural contexts, 
discusses stylistic dating, and compares them with other Aegean signet rings. 
The second section expands on the religious and eschatological interpretations 
of the imagery on the rings, framed as a \"glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world.\" The digitized volume itself must be consulted page by page or via
the downloadable PDF to extract precise statements about when and where the 
treasure, and specifically the Ring of Nestor, was discovered and by whom."
