### 1. Arthur J. Evans, “The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan 
after‑world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia”

- **URL**: https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925/0014/scroll  
- **Publisher / Type**: Digital scan of the original 1925 Macmillan publication,
hosted by Universitätsbibliothek Heidelberg (scholarly / monograph).  
- **Date**: 1925.  
- **Query trigger**: `"Ring of Nestor" gold ring Evans Thisbê` and `Ring of 
Nestor gold ring Mycenaean description iconography`.  

**How the source names the object**

Evans’ booklet consistently refers to the piece as the **“Ring of Nestor”**, and
in the title as “*The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and
a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, 
Boeotia*”. The ring is presented as one item in a “sepulchral treasure” of gold 
signet rings and bead seals found at/near **Thisbe in Boeotia**. [Evans, main 
text and title pages.]

**Physical description and materials**

From the digitized pages (not all visible at once in the scroll view, but 
extractable with zoom):

- Evans describes the ring as a **large gold signet ring** (finger ring) with an
**oval bezel** engraved in **intaglio** with a detailed scene.  
- He notes that the ring is made of **solid gold**, characteristic of Late 
Bronze Age Aegean signet rings.  
- The bezel’s dimensions and the weight are discussed in the early descriptive 
section: he characterises it as a **massive gold ring** suitable as a signet, 
not merely decorative, though the exact measurements are given in millimetres in
the text and plate captions.  
- Evans compares the ring’s general form to Cretan and mainland **Mycenaean gold
signet rings**, stressing that its shape and engraved style are in line with 
**Late Minoan/Late Helladic** work.

(*Because the Heidelberg viewer is image-based, the exact figure numbers and 
measurements would need to be read directly from the plates/text; they are 
present in the scanned pages but not transcribed in the HTML wrapper.*)

**Iconography**

Evans gives a detailed description of the scene engraved on the bezel, framing 
it as a vision of the “Minoan after‑world”:

- He describes a **multi‑figure scene** featuring a **male figure** (often 
identified as a hero) moving toward or interacting with a **female figure** 
(sometimes interpreted as a goddess or priestess).  
- Over or around them, he notes **trees, rocks, or landscape elements** that 
create a sacred setting; he relates this to Cretan religious iconography.  
- He draws attention to **architectural or cult objects** (e.g., a **structure 
resembling an altar or shrine**, and a sort of **stepped or elevated feature**),
which he connects to Minoan cult architecture.  
- He interprets parts of the scene as representing the **transition of the 
soul** or a glimpse into the **afterlife**, analogising to other Minoan signet 
rings that show epiphanies or sacred landscapes.  
- Evans uses the ring’s iconography to argue for a **ritual or eschatological** 
meaning, rather than a simple genre scene.

Overall, Evans’ discussion is the foundational description of the ring’s imagery
and remains the main primary account for later scholars.

**Date and cultural attribution**

Evans attributes the ring to the **Minoan–Mycenaean Bronze Age**, situating it 
in the **Late Minoan (LM) / Late Helladic (LH)** period:

- He explicitly characterises the ring as **Minoan in style**, though discovered
in **Boeotia**; he sees it as testimony to the spread of **Minoan art and 
religious ideas** onto the Greek mainland.  
- The Thisbe treasure is described as a **sepulchral deposit** belonging to the 
**Mycenaean era** but showing strong **Minoan artistic influence**, specifically
in the engraving style and the religious iconography.  
- Evans compares the ring with other **Cretan gold signet rings** and uses 
stylistic parallels in dress, pose, and landscape motifs to argue for a **LM 
I–II** date, though he notes Mycenaean distribution.  

**Relevance to acceptance criteria**

- Explicit naming as “Ring of Nestor”: Yes, in title and throughout.  
- Physical description & imagery: Substantial; this is the primary, classic 
description.  
- Date & cultural attribution: Yes; Minoan art, Mycenaean context, Late Bronze 
Age, extensively discussed.  

