{"query": {"id": "20260628162109496264_3d28ea88", "handle": "q1", "text": "Ring 
of Nestor gold ring Mycenaean description iconography", "importance": "core"}, 
"items": [{"id": "20260628162413060451_b9212421", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"The title page of Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph reads: “The ring of Nestor: 
a glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413140135_aa574e47", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In the descriptive
section, Evans refers to the object as “the Ring of Nestor” and states that it 
formed part of a “sepulchral treasure” of gold signet rings and bead‑seals 
discovered at or near Thisbê in Boeotia.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413222365_5832e902", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans describes 
the ring as a large gold signet with an oval bezel engraved in intaglio, noting 
that it is a “massive” piece of solid gold characteristic of Late Bronze Age 
Aegean signet rings.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], 
"citations": [7]}, {"id": "20260628162413306120_8f70f14d", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans’s discussion of the engraved scene mentions a male figure 
approaching or interacting with a female figure, with trees or rocks forming a 
sacred landscape and architectural or cult objects resembling altars or shrines 
included in the composition.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], 
"citations": [7]}, {"id": "20260628162413384645_d3011627", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans characterises the scene on the ring as offering “a glimpse into 
the Minoan after‑world” and argues that it represents a ritual or eschatological
event rather than a simple everyday scene.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413460547_3503386b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In comparing the 
ring to other Cretan gold signets, Evans notes stylistic parallels in dress, 
poses, and landscape motifs and uses these to argue for a Late Minoan I–II date 
within the broader Late Helladic period.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413536896_379c8104", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans explicitly 
states that the Thisbê treasure, including the Ring of Nestor, is Mycenaean in 
archaeological context but exhibits strong Minoan artistic influence.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413617804_62602cf5", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Nature 
review’s heading reproduces the book’s title: “The Ring of Nestor: a Glimpse 
into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and 
Bead-Seals from Thisbe, Boeotia.”", "source_ids": 
["20260628162410719452_89618ff5"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413695205_a4efda4d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Childe remarks 
that Evans has revealed “a prehistoric Minoan and Mycenaean stratum beneath 
classical Greek civilisation” and that the Ring of Nestor provides a glimpse 
into a Minoan after‑world influencing later Greek religion.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162410719452_89618ff5"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413776988_3a427108", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The title of 
Theodore Eliopoulos’s article reads “Some observations on the iconography of the
‘Ring of Nestor’,” clearly identifying the subject artefact.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413854944_7c212dba", "type": "Evidence", "text": "According to the 
article description, Eliopoulos analyses specific iconographic elements on the 
Ring of Nestor, such as incurved altars and other cult motifs, and compares them
with Minoan and Mycenaean signet imagery to reassess the scene’s meaning.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628162413936642_a452fde8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same 
description notes that Eliopoulos engages with earlier archaeological 
interpretations of the Ring of Nestor and highlights differences between his 
conclusions and those of previous scholars.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628162414020569_c4fe7354", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Academia page 
indicates that Eliopoulos addresses the authenticity debate concerning the Ring 
of Nestor and argues that iconographic correspondences such as incurved altars 
support considering the ring a genuine Minoan–Mycenaean artefact.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628162414096590_c19f7ca9", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The ResearchGate 
entry lists the article title as “The ‘Ring of Nestor’” and states that it 
presents “new technical observations” which support the authenticity of the Ring
of Nestor.", "source_ids": ["20260628162410770994_8e4b2786"], "citations": 
[10]}, {"id": "20260628162414170928_dedd525a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
same abstract notes that “in 1995 the ring was once again placed on display,” 
indicating a re‑display of the Ring of Nestor in that year.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162410770994_8e4b2786"], "citations": [10]}, {"id": 
"20260628162414247717_94202c4b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The HathiTrust 
catalog record gives the full title of Evans’s book as “The ring of Nestor: a 
glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia” and lists Arthur J. Evans as 
the author.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224726379_c2ff62df"], "citations": 
[8]}, {"id": "20260628162414330062_bf4aa2c6", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
catalog record includes subject headings such as “Minoan civilization,” “Signet 
rings,” and “Boeotia (Greece) – Thisbe,” associating the work with those 
topics.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224726379_c2ff62df"], "citations": [8]}, 
{"id": "20260628162414418181_68516878", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Archaeological Society at Athens study notes that Mycenaean signet rings are 
typically made of solid gold with engraved bezels in intaglio and often depict 
sacred scenes involving altars and landscape motifs.", "source_ids": 
["20260628162410831356_350b5dc6"], "citations": [11]}, {"id": 
"20260628162414494058_9eaed8db", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The same study 
remarks that many Mycenaean signet rings show stylistic and iconographic 
influence from Minoan Crete, particularly in their religious imagery and 
composition.", "source_ids": ["20260628162410831356_350b5dc6"], "citations": 
[11]}, {"id": "20260628162438170047_255d291f", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
gold signet commonly called the Ring of Nestor is a large, massive finger ring 
of solid gold, with an oval bezel engraved in intaglio bearing a complex 
multi‑figure scene that includes a male and a female figure in a sacred 
landscape with trees or rocks and cult or architectural objects resembling 
altars or shrines.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83", 
"20260628162410831356_350b5dc6"], "citations": [7, 11]}, {"id": 
"20260628162438321873_8ca9c716", "type": "Claim", "text": "The object studied by
Evans and later scholars is explicitly designated as the Ring of Nestor in early
and modern publications, including Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph \"The ring 
of Nestor\" and Theodore Eliopoulos’s article \"Some observations on the 
iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’\".", "source_ids": 
["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf", "20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83", 
"20260628162224726379_c2ff62df", "20260628162410719452_89618ff5"], "citations": 
[3, 7, 8, 9]}, {"id": "20260628162438542535_eae9bb22", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Evans reports that the Ring of Nestor formed part of a sepulchral treasure of 
gold signet rings and bead seals discovered at or near Thisbe in Boeotia in a 
Mycenaean funerary context.", "source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83", 
"20260628162224726379_c2ff62df"], "citations": [7, 8]}, {"id": 
"20260628162438758330_f67c1ec2", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans dates the Ring 
of Nestor and the associated Thisbe gold treasure to the Late Bronze Age, 
specifically to Late Minoan I–II within the broader Late Helladic period, on the
basis of stylistic parallels in dress, poses, and landscape motifs with Cretan 
and mainland Mycenaean gold signet rings; later writers such as Eliopoulos and 
Childe also treat it as a Late Bronze Age, prehistoric Aegean artefact.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf", "20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83",
"20260628162410719452_89618ff5"], "citations": [3, 7, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628162438918292_36058f3b", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans and subsequent 
scholars attribute the Ring of Nestor to a Mycenaean archaeological context 
strongly influenced by Minoan art and religion, situating it within a 
Minoan–Mycenaean cultural sphere and using it as evidence for the spread of 
Minoan artistic styles and religious ideas to the Greek mainland.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf", "20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83",
"20260628162224726379_c2ff62df", "20260628162410719452_89618ff5", 
"20260628162410770994_8e4b2786", "20260628162410831356_350b5dc6"], "citations": 
[3, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11]}, {"id": "20260628162439264689_5ae2a191", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "Evans interprets the engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor as offering a
glimpse into the Minoan after‑world and representing a ritual or eschatological 
event, while later commentators such as Childe use this interpretation to argue 
that the ring provides evidence for a prehistoric Minoan–Mycenaean religious 
stratum underlying and influencing later classical Greek religion.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162224696610_c9b6bb83", 
"20260628162410719452_89618ff5"], "citations": [7, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628162439495442_8576b217", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern scholarship 
addresses a debate over whether the Ring of Nestor is an ancient artefact or a 
modern forgery; studies such as Theodore Eliopoulos’s and a technical article 
titled \"The ‘Ring of Nestor’\" present new iconographic and technical 
observations, including correspondences like incurved altars, that argue in 
favour of the ring’s authenticity as a genuine Minoan–Mycenaean Aegean gold 
signet ring.", "source_ids": ["20260628162220158891_7c0d11cf", 
"20260628162410770994_8e4b2786"], "citations": [3, 10]}, {"id": 
"20260628162439831192_8be903a8", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor 
has a documented modern museum display history, including being (re)placed on 
public display in 1995 in a museum collection, although the specific institution
and inventory number are not specified in the available abstract.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628162410770994_8e4b2786"], "citations": [10]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 50, "total": 27, "next_offset": null}
